WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.834
-Take the love, a standing O
for Edward Norton.

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:05.067
Come on, bud. Welcome back.
I missed having you on the show.

00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:06.601
-I know. It's great,
great to be back.

00:00:06.601 --> 00:00:07.767
I feel like
we're back alive again.

00:00:07.767 --> 00:00:10.000
-Yeah, it feels right.
Everyone, we got crowds.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.434
We have audiences
in the building.

00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:13.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:16.367
We just had puppies predict
March Madness.

00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:19.000
And I thought since, staying
with the basketball theme,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.767
I was going to talk to you
a little bit about basketball.

00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:22.100
And then I was doing --
I remembered

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.100
that you dunked famously
in the movies,

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.934
in "American History X."

00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:30.100
This is you.
This is you dunking the ball.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.734
You. Not many people can say
they dunked on film before,

00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:34.033
but you have.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.734
Do you remember
getting air on this?

00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:39.133
-There's a lot to say about
this.

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:40.501
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.501 --> 00:00:43.968
I was very pumped up on,
you know, for that film.

00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:45.901
-You were jacked.
-And everybody thought

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:50.067
that equated to vertical
jumping capability, right?

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:51.267
So we get out there
to shoot it.

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:52.567
They're going to do
these special shots

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:56.801
to get the dunk.
-Where are you filming it?

00:00:56.801 --> 00:00:58.868
-In the Venice basketball
courts. Right?

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:00.734
So there's a lot
of real players all around

00:01:00.734 --> 00:01:02.167
and everything like that.

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:05.934
And they say, "Where
do you want us to set the rim?"

00:01:05.934 --> 00:01:09.834
And I said, "I don't know.
You know, nine feet.

00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:11.667
Nine feet. That should be good,
right?"

00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.734
-Yeah, that's my dude!
That's Edward Norton.

00:01:13.734 --> 00:01:15.367
-No, it didn't go well.
It didn't go well.

00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:16.734
[ Laughter ]

00:01:16.734 --> 00:01:18.868
Look, I was prison body on top

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:23.000
and like legs of a of a Kenyan
marathon runner on the bottom.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.133
They put me in sweatpants
to hide the differential

00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:28.667
between up here and down here.
So I missed.

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:32.167
So I'm like -- I'm banging the
ball up against the underside

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.767
at nine feet.
-You're dunking up.

00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:36.234
-So then the worst
possible thing,

00:01:36.234 --> 00:01:39.200
which is they have to lower it
in front of everybody, right?

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.634
So I'm already
catching [bleep]

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.767
They lower it
and it's still not enough.

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.200
And I'm getting more and more
tired. At the end of the day,

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.133
basically, I put my hand
as high as I could reach.

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:50.667
They put the rim at my wrist...

00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:52.300
[ Laughter ]

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.133
And basically my --

00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:58.434
-This is like a Nerf hoop on the
back of a closet somewhere

00:01:58.434 --> 00:01:59.667
in a bedroom.

00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:02.901
-I'm standing
on the ground in that shot.

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:04.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:06.167
That is the honest --
-Movie magic.

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:09.334
-And actually I'm getting
embarrassed

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:11.701
at the memory
of the catcalls.

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.467
-Sorry. Sorry.
-So can we go to something...

00:02:13.467 --> 00:02:15.400
-Yes, of course we can. Yes, we
can go right to the next.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.634
-...I did well in a film?
-Let's talk about "Glass Onion,"

00:02:17.634 --> 00:02:19.400
by the way,
because that was gigantic.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.868
You were great.
You were fantastic in that.

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:25.734
When we first meet
your character, Miles,

00:02:25.734 --> 00:02:28.467
Miles Bron,
you're playing guitar,

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:29.968
and I know
you play some guitar,

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:31.534
but you were playing
"Blackbird" pretty well.

00:02:31.534 --> 00:02:32.868
I was like,
"Is that really you?"

00:02:32.868 --> 00:02:34.167
Or did you lower the rim
on this one?

00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:36.501
[ Laughter ]
-No. So that --

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:39.100
Like you,
I'm an amateur guitar player.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.534
I can play "Blackbird."

00:02:41.534 --> 00:02:44.434
And I think maybe
that's why I got the part,

00:02:44.434 --> 00:02:46.000
because he was supposed
to be playing

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.701
"Blackbird"
specifically at that point.

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:49.801
And Rian Johnson,
the director, said,

00:02:49.801 --> 00:02:52.634
"Can you play it?"
I said, "Actually, I can."

00:02:52.634 --> 00:02:56.200
So I -- So yes,
I was playing it in that.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.167
The thing is, though,

00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:00.634
I don't -- You sit in
sometimes, right?

00:03:00.634 --> 00:03:02.334
Like, you -- I remember you

00:03:02.334 --> 00:03:04.000
at the "Saturday Night Live"
40th party.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.467
You were sitting in.
But I think if you play guitar

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:11.467
a little, you know,
I have these dreams

00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:14.033
like where Springsteen
has called me on stage

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:17.200
and I'm failing.
Do you have guitar-fail dreams?

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.367
-Oh, my gosh. I have just
nothing but fail dreams.

00:03:19.367 --> 00:03:20.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:22.934
Made a career out of it.

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:24.567
That's why I'm a comedian.

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:26.734
-But the funny thing --
and the funny thing on that one

00:03:26.734 --> 00:03:28.834
was because there's this --

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:30.000
Enough people
have seen the movie.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.567
I don't think it ruins it
that my character

00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:34.000
is kind of revealed
to be a pretentious idiot.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.467
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:03:35.467 --> 00:03:37.634
But we -- I had this --

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:41.501
You're a Beatles aficionado.
I really wanted that --

00:03:41.501 --> 00:03:43.901
She comes up and she says,
"You're playing my song."

00:03:43.901 --> 00:03:45.934
And he says, yeah, on the guitar
he wrote it on.

00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:48.934
And it's supposed to be like
he actually bought things.

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:52.267
And I said to Rian, "What if --
Can I say, like, on the guitar

00:03:52.267 --> 00:03:55.267
John wrote it on"
because McCartney wrote it,

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:56.734
and this would be good
because like --

00:03:56.734 --> 00:03:58.667
-Inside.
-Yeah, inside baseball

00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:00.801
but another level on which
he's an idiot, right?

00:04:00.801 --> 00:04:01.868
I thought it'd be great.

00:04:01.868 --> 00:04:04.267
And basically Rian was like,

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:06.067
"I'm not getting into
the McCartney-Lennon

00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:08.400
authorship wars."
He's like, "No."

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.567
And I said, "Paul will get it.
Paul is cool."

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:13.400
-Yeah. He's like, "No."

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.400
By the way, the fact that Paul
said yes to even letting

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.968
"Glass Onion" be used,
I'm like,

00:04:16.968 --> 00:04:19.067
dude, that was just --
it was masterful.

00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:20.501
That's hilarious.
You tried to get nerdy.

00:04:20.501 --> 00:04:22.100
-I thought it would be a good
joke to attribute

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:23.267
"Blackbird" to John Lennon.

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:24.701
-I found a photo that I wanted
to show you

00:04:24.701 --> 00:04:26.701
that I think this is
actually -- you set it up

00:04:26.701 --> 00:04:28.400
perfectly without even knowing
I'm going to show you this,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.701
but this is a photo of you from
I think this was 2001.

00:04:31.701 --> 00:04:33.467
Here's Paul McCartney
playing guitar,

00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:36.267
Eric Clapton behind him.
And in between, a very...

00:04:36.267 --> 00:04:38.234
[ Laughter ]

00:04:38.234 --> 00:04:40.667
Are you having a nightmare?

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:42.434
[ Laughter ]

00:04:42.434 --> 00:04:44.167
That's two of the
greatest guitarists.

00:04:44.167 --> 00:04:46.434
-Where do you even find that?
-Dude, do you remember?

00:04:46.434 --> 00:04:47.901
-Well, it's an incredibly
weird picture

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:49.434
because it looks like
I'm trying to tune

00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:51.334
Clapton's guitar
while he's playing it.

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:53.234
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:55.367
It's like, "You're a little
flat. You're a little flat."

00:04:55.367 --> 00:04:57.000
-"Mr. Eric, come here.
I'll help you with that."

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.901
-But also, I'm sitting here
thinking, like,

00:04:58.901 --> 00:05:03.267
did I have such a hit movie that
I felt I belonged on this stage?

00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:04.834
-Yes, you do!
-With Clapton and things.

00:05:04.834 --> 00:05:06.000
Like, what is going on?

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.467
-I've seen you play
with Eddie Vedder.

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:10.467
Was that --
I saw you somewhere.

00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:12.634
-I sat in once
with Eddie Vedder.

00:05:12.634 --> 00:05:13.968
-So you know
what you're talking about.

00:05:13.968 --> 00:05:17.734
-No, no. It was the
most nerve-wracking performance

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:20.834
experience of my life
because he said,

00:05:20.834 --> 00:05:22.400
"Why don't you just sit in
for one song?" So I said,

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.300
Let's play 'Guaranteed.'"
This was at the Beacon.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.534
This was at our hometown
theater here, right?

00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:27.367
-Yeah. New York.

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:28.567
-I'm like, "Let's play
'Guaranteed.'"

00:05:28.567 --> 00:05:30.200
I know "Guaranteed" pretty well,
right?

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.534
Right before we go out
and do it --

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:34.667
and his fans
are intense, man --

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.067
You don't mess with
like the Eddie --

00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:37.968
I was like, "I could get booed."

00:05:37.968 --> 00:05:39.400
-Oh, yeah.
-I go out, right?

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.734
I'm like, "Okay, I can play

00:05:40.734 --> 00:05:43.701
'Guaranteed' well enough
that this will go fine."

00:05:43.701 --> 00:05:45.901
And then he's like --
right before he goes,

00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:47.767
"Take a verse, take a verse."

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:49.367
I'm like,
"I'm not singing with you."

00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:51.234
-No! I'm barely doing this.

00:05:51.234 --> 00:05:52.667
-He's like, "Take a verse.
Take a verse."

00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:55.167
And Eddie's voice
sounds like it's rising

00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:56.801
from the mantle of the Earth.

00:05:56.801 --> 00:05:58.567
You know, it's like
this huge thing.

00:05:58.567 --> 00:06:01.067
And I opened my mouth and
it's like Bob Dylan

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:04.334
and Ellie Goulding have had
a had a baby, right?

00:06:04.334 --> 00:06:06.767
It's just a disaster.
It's like you don't want to

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:08.501
sing next to Eddie Vedder.

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:09.601
-You just want
to tune his guitar.

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:10.868
That's all you want to do.
-Exactly.

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:12.434
So it's just like you
got to be very careful

00:06:12.434 --> 00:06:13.968
about sitting in
with the pros.

00:06:13.968 --> 00:06:15.567
-"Extrapolations."

00:06:15.567 --> 00:06:17.534
We have to talk about
your show "Extrapolations."

00:06:17.534 --> 00:06:19.667
The cast, by the way, is --
Yeah, the show is --

00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:22.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.000
It's on Apple TV+.
The cast.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.300
I'm just going to run through
some names here.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.667
No order. Sienna Miller.
Kit Harington.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:32.434
David Schwimmer. Daveed Diggs.
Tobey Maguire. Forest Whitaker.

00:06:32.434 --> 00:06:34.200
Murray Bartlett.
Meryl Streep.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:35.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:35.968 --> 00:06:38.234
-Marion Cotillard.
-Marion Cotillard.

00:06:38.234 --> 00:06:40.534
-Yeah. They said Meryl Streep,
Marion Cotillard.

00:06:40.534 --> 00:06:42.200
I was like, "I don't care what
it's about."

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.934
-"I'm in."
-Right.

00:06:43.934 --> 00:06:45.634
And then I didn't get to work
with either of them.

00:06:45.634 --> 00:06:48.501
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. You were saying this.

00:06:48.501 --> 00:06:50.767
-It's like an all star --

00:06:50.767 --> 00:06:52.434
I think a way to look
at this is,

00:06:52.434 --> 00:06:56.267
is it's sort of like the --
if you like "Black Mirror,"

00:06:56.267 --> 00:06:58.901
you know, the way that "Black
Mirror" comments on technology,

00:06:58.901 --> 00:07:00.634
which I was obsessed
with that show.

00:07:00.634 --> 00:07:02.934
This is very much
like a "Black Mirror"

00:07:02.934 --> 00:07:06.634
for future environmental
kind of consequence

00:07:06.634 --> 00:07:09.133
and catastrophe.
It's really, really creative.

00:07:09.133 --> 00:07:11.901
It's rooted in science, but
it's -- Some of them are funny.

00:07:11.901 --> 00:07:14.400
Some of them
are really dramatic

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.567
and kind of terrifying
and dystopian.

00:07:16.567 --> 00:07:20.901
And it just -- I think that
it's entertaining as hell,

00:07:20.901 --> 00:07:23.334
But it's a really
great provocation

00:07:23.334 --> 00:07:26.801
to get us to think
about what might actually

00:07:26.801 --> 00:07:29.400
be coming
on an environmental level,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.300
not just a technological level.
-Since I've known you,

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.567
you've always been
active in the environment,

00:07:33.567 --> 00:07:35.133
saving the environment
and, you know,

00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:38.400
watching out for climate change
and all this stuff.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.000
-Yeah, honestly, it's
nice when the thing you do --

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.968
when you have the opportunity
to wrestle

00:07:44.968 --> 00:07:48.133
with some of those complicated
questions and challenges

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:52.200
that we're facing
within the work.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.334
I mean, I think --
-It's still entertaining.

00:07:53.334 --> 00:07:54.667
-Yeah, it is entertaining.

00:07:54.667 --> 00:07:56.934
It's very entertaining,
but it's not preachy.

00:07:56.934 --> 00:08:00.234
It really basically
deals with how difficult

00:08:00.234 --> 00:08:03.067
this is going to be to make
these complicated decisions.

00:08:03.067 --> 00:08:05.400
-And how life is going
to be in the future.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.567
It starts in when, 2037?

00:08:07.567 --> 00:08:11.400
-Yeah. And it advances
through time as the episodes go.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.934
But I think, you know,
look, we live in a very --

00:08:15.934 --> 00:08:21.300
we live in an age of distraction
and of a lot --

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.634
There's a lot of challenges
to life.

00:08:22.634 --> 00:08:26.100
And I think
the most it asks of people

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.067
is really that, you know,
this is --

00:08:29.067 --> 00:08:31.234
this is going to be
the challenge of our era.

00:08:31.234 --> 00:08:33.601
I think we will be
remembered mostly

00:08:33.601 --> 00:08:37.868
for how we deal with this issue
of ecological sustainability

00:08:37.868 --> 00:08:40.167
and whether we
set things in motion

00:08:40.167 --> 00:08:42.434
that future generations
are dealing with.

00:08:42.434 --> 00:08:46.501
And I think all this
really asks you to do is --

00:08:46.501 --> 00:08:49.734
is not be complacent,
you know, to at least meditate

00:08:49.734 --> 00:08:51.868
on the consequences
of these decisions

00:08:51.868 --> 00:08:54.834
that we're making right now
and think a little bit about

00:08:54.834 --> 00:08:57.133
what it's actually going to
look like if we don't act.

00:08:57.133 --> 00:08:59.767
[ Applause ]

00:08:59.767 --> 00:09:01.567
-What would you say
for someone

00:09:01.567 --> 00:09:05.067
who wants to get
get involved or --

00:09:05.067 --> 00:09:08.968
-You know, again, it's hard
to -- I mean, in the old days,

00:09:08.968 --> 00:09:11.234
like, I think we liked
hearing things that,

00:09:11.234 --> 00:09:13.133
you know, changing light bulbs
and this and that.

00:09:13.133 --> 00:09:14.968
But we know that
that's not -- those things

00:09:14.968 --> 00:09:16.234
aren't really what it's about.

00:09:16.234 --> 00:09:18.701
It's about policy.
It's about governments.

00:09:18.701 --> 00:09:20.567
And that's why
a lot of people feel

00:09:20.567 --> 00:09:22.767
that it's beyond
their capability.

00:09:22.767 --> 00:09:24.634
I think we've got to vote.

00:09:24.634 --> 00:09:27.000
We've got to bring
in a generation of leaders

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.400
who put this -- who believe
that this is a priority

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:33.000
and make it a priority.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.067
But also, I just think,
you know,

00:09:35.067 --> 00:09:38.334
I think of -- There's a line
of Hemingway's

00:09:38.334 --> 00:09:40.467
that's cited in the episode

00:09:40.467 --> 00:09:43.334
I'm in that the world
is a fine place

00:09:43.334 --> 00:09:44.834
and worth fighting for.

00:09:44.834 --> 00:09:49.334
And I think that we've
got to push against complacency.

00:09:49.334 --> 00:09:52.267
I think we can afford
as a generation and our kids,

00:09:52.267 --> 00:09:53.701
we can't be complacent
about this.

00:09:53.701 --> 00:09:56.434
It's too consequential.
-Yeah. Good for you.

00:09:56.434 --> 00:09:58.033
[ Cheers and applause ]
Well said.

00:09:58.033 --> 00:09:59.801
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:09:59.801 --> 00:10:02.267
Here's Edward Norton
in "Extrapolations."

00:10:02.267 --> 00:10:03.567
Take a look at this.

00:10:03.567 --> 00:10:05.100
-The real reckless experiment

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.367
is the one civilization
has been running.

00:10:07.367 --> 00:10:09.934
-Nobody was trying
to change the climate.

00:10:09.934 --> 00:10:12.234
We didn't even think
that we had that capacity.

00:10:12.234 --> 00:10:14.667
We didn't know better.
But we do now.

00:10:14.667 --> 00:10:19.300
-And yet human behavior
doesn't change.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:22.601
The sky is easier to re-engineer
than the human brain, John.

00:10:22.601 --> 00:10:23.868
-Exactly.

00:10:23.868 --> 00:10:26.934
And if you tell
that greedy monkey brain,

00:10:26.934 --> 00:10:28.701
it's cooled off
and everything's okay,

00:10:28.701 --> 00:10:29.934
it's going to say,
"Give me a little bit more.

00:10:29.934 --> 00:10:31.400
Just let me burn
a little bit more.

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:33.801
Just let me -- Just let me
mortgage a little more

00:10:33.801 --> 00:10:36.000
of the children's future
so that I can make sure

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.601
I can choose
this quarterly balance sheet.

00:10:37.601 --> 00:10:40.067
I just need that one."
It's -- It's -- It's --

00:10:40.067 --> 00:10:41.901
Gita, Gita.

00:10:41.901 --> 00:10:44.868
Even if you got all of the
chemical interactions right,

00:10:44.868 --> 00:10:47.100
if you got the
dispersal pattern right,

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:50.467
the problem here is bigger
than the heat of the sun, Gita.

00:10:50.467 --> 00:10:53.334
It's the way we see the world.

00:10:53.334 --> 00:10:56.501
-Edward Norton, everybody!
"Extrapolations"

00:10:56.501 --> 00:10:59.267
is streaming now
on Apple TV+.

00:10:59.267 --> 00:11:01.200
We'll be right back
with Ego Nwodim.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:02.634
Stick around, everybody.

